{
  "description": "The agent console, populated. `canonical-board.json` with 007's three agent-written regions filled in — an active ticket, an update stream and a prompt shelf.\n\n**It is a second file rather than an addition to the canonical board, and that is the point.** Those three regions are empty until an agent calls something, and three end-to-end tests assert exactly that: they check the empty states say what fills them rather than looking broken (FR-141), which is the state a fresh install is actually in. Populating the canonical board turned all three red. The empty board and the full one are both real states worth having a fixture for, so there are two.\n\nThe active ticket is MERC-1184 while the session reports against MERC-1190, deliberately: a session's work item and the operator's focus are different pointers, and a board that conflated them would look correct against a fixture where they agreed.\n\n**Not here: the handed-off tickets T150 also asks for.** That lane was 007 M2 and it was dropped on 2026-08-20 without being built — it needed JQL history operators verified against a Jira nobody could reach. There are no rows to seed because there is no lane to seed them into.\n\n**Also here: the ticket history 008 adds.** Three entries, one of them against MERC-1150 — a ticket no lane holds and the mirror has never seen. That is the case the feature exists for and the one nothing else on this board can represent: it has **no** `ticketSummary`, because the seeder records it the way an agent would and the mirror has nothing to snapshot. Live tickets get one; this one shows what an entry looks like when the snapshot was never taken. The retention rule itself — a snapshot kept after the ticket goes — is asserted in `test/services/history.test.ts`, where the ticket can be made to leave.\n\nEvery timestamp is an offset resolved at load (FR-118).",
